    "en":  {
               "caution":  "Volatility is extreme, and one 22.63% relief rally does not reverse a steep downtrend where overhead resistance sits at $135.04 and $159.53. With Q2 FY2027 earnings landing on December 1, 2026, expectations are elevated around $525M–$535M revenue and 67%–69% non-GAAP gross margins; any hint of hyperscaler order digestion, slower AEC demand, or margin dilution from DustPhotonics integration could trigger another sharp drop. Heavy top-four customer concentration (84%) and October insider sales under 10b5-1 plans warrant disciplined risk management.",
               "cons":  [
                            "Remains in a bearish downtrend at $122.46, trading well below its EMA 50 ($159.53) and EMA 200 ($175.30).",
                            "Top four hyperscaler customers represented 84% of Q1 FY2027 revenue (33%, 28%, 13%, 10%), creating heavy concentration risk.",
                            "High-beta swings erased roughly 60% from its $308.67 52-week high, with Stifel trimming its target to $250 on November 20, 2026.",
                            "Top executives sold tens of millions in shares during October 2026, mostly under pre-planned 10b5-1 schedules, adding sentiment drag."
                        ],
               "fundamental":  "Credo supplies essential high-speed copper and optical plumbing linking GPUs and switches inside AI clusters—spanning Active Electrical Cables (AECs), SerDes chips, and optical DSPs. Execution has been exceptional: in Q1 FY2027 (ended August 1, 2026), revenue surged 114.7% YoY to $479.0M with 68.0% non-GAAP gross margin and $236.3M non-GAAP net income ($1.20 diluted EPS). Management guided Q2 FY2027 (due December 1, 2026) to $525M–$535M and full-year FY2027 growth above 85%, including over $600M from optics as May 2026\u0027s DustPhotonics acquisition adds in-house silicon photonics PICs. Recent developments reinforce rather than weaken the moat: the November 17 Marvell settlement clears AEC patent challenges, while November 4 OCP approval of ZeroFlap optical telemetry with Oracle strengthens system-level lock-in. The stock\u0027s drawdown reflects sector multiple compression rather than business deterioration, though 84% revenue concentration in four hyperscalers remains a structural vulnerability.",
               "headline":  "CRDO rebounds 22.6% off oversold lows, but trend stays bearish before Dec 1 earnings",
               "news":  [
                            "Nov 23, 2026: CRDO surged 22.63% to $122.46 on 10.65M shares during a broad AI semiconductor relief rally and pre-earnings dip buying, partially reversing a steep mid-November selloff.",
                            "Nov 20, 2026: Stifel maintained its Buy rating on CRDO but cut its price target from $310 to $250 amid AI infrastructure valuation pressure as shares fell roughly 14%–15% alongside networking peers.",
                            "Nov 17, 2026: Credo and Marvell Technology announced a confidential settlement resolving their Active Electrical Cable (AEC) patent disputes, terminating Marvell\u0027s pending USPTO challenges against Credo\u0027s patents.",
                            "Nov 11, 2026: Credo scheduled its Q2 FY2027 financial results and conference call for December 1, 2026 after market close, with prior revenue guidance set at $525M–$535M.",
                            "Nov 4, 2026: Credo and Oracle announced Open Compute Project (OCP) approval of two optical transceiver telemetry specifications based on Credo\u0027s ZeroFlap framework for 400G, 800G, and 1.6T links."
                        ],
               "opportunity":  "For long-term AI infrastructure investors, the disconnect between a 60% drawdown from the $308.67 high and strengthening fundamentals stands out. Clearing Marvell\u0027s AEC patent challenges on November 17 removes a key legal overhang, while Oracle and OCP\u0027s November 4 adoption of ZeroFlap optical telemetry validates Credo\u0027s push from copper cables into vertically integrated optical systems. If December 1 results confirm $525M+ quarterly revenue, resilient high-60% margins, and steady optical momentum toward $600M+ in FY2027, holding above the $97.63 lower band could support longer-term base-building.",
               "opportunityCheck":  "The current sub-scores largely fit Credo\u0027s high AI revenue exposure and fast market growth, backed by triple-digit revenue expansion. However, optionality looks slightly understated and warrants moving higher: integrating DustPhotonics\u0027 silicon photonics (first revenue booked in Q1 FY2027), targeting over $600M in FY2027 optical sales, and gaining November 4 Oracle/OCP approval for ZeroFlap telemetry show a credible second engine beyond copper AECs. Keeping market position and bottleneck moderate still makes sense—the November 17 Marvell settlement eases IP risk, yet fierce rivals and 84% top-four customer concentration cap unilateral pricing power.",
               "pros":  [
                            "Settled its AEC patent dispute with Marvell on November 17, 2026, ending pending USPTO patent challenges.",
                            "Posted $479.0M Q1 FY2027 revenue (+114.7% YoY) with 68.0% non-GAAP gross margin and guided Q2 to $525M–$535M.",
                            "Won OCP approval on November 4, 2026 alongside Oracle for two optical telemetry standards built on Credo\u0027s ZeroFlap framework.",
                            "Expanded into silicon photonics via the May 2026 DustPhotonics deal, targeting over $600M in FY2027 optical revenue."
                        ],
               "risks":  [
                             "Extreme customer concentration: top four hyperscalers generated 84% of Q1 FY2027 revenue (33%, 28%, 13%, 10%).",
                             "Intensifying interconnect competition from Broadcom, Marvell, and Astera Labs as copper and optical links scale toward 1.6T.",
                             "Execution and margin risk integrating DustPhotonics\u0027 silicon photonics operations while scaling ZeroFlap optical systems.",
                             "High-beta valuation sensitivity to AI capex-cycle jitters, underscored by the 60% pullback from the $308.67 52-week high."
                         ],
               "summary":  "Credo Technology (NASDAQ:CRDO) jumped 22.63% to $122.46 on November 23, 2026, snapping back from a sector-wide AI chip selloff, yet it remains 60% below its $308.67 52-week high and in a bearish trend below all key moving averages. Fundamentally, the long-term moat stays intact: on November 17 Credo settled its AEC patent dispute with Marvell, and on November 4 Oracle and the OCP approved Credo\u0027s ZeroFlap optical telemetry standards. Investors now watch December 1 Q2 FY2027 results against $525M–$535M guidance and 84% top-four customer concentration.",
               "technical":  "Despite surging 22.63% to $122.46 on November 23, 2026, CRDO\u0027s technical posture remains bearish. Price sits far below its three exponential moving averages (EMAs—weighted trend averages): EMA 50 ($159.53), EMA 200 ($175.30), and EMA 100 ($176.51). With EMA 50 well under EMA 200 (a bearish death cross) and EMA 100 converging toward EMA 200, the multi-month trend points down. Momentum is stabilizing but still negative: MACD (12/26/9, a trend-momentum gauge) reads -15.31 versus its -15.06 signal line, though the narrow -0.25 histogram hints selling pressure is easing. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) recovered to 42.6, exiting oversold territory yet staying under the neutral 50 mark. On volatility, CRDO bounced cleanly off its lower Bollinger Band ($97.63—two standard deviations below the 20-day average) but stays beneath the $135.04 middle band and 60.3% below its $308.67 52-week high (versus $86.49 low). Volume of 10.65M shares ran 27% above the 8.41M 20-day average, confirming bounce interest, though closing below the $126.12 open shows supply. Key support sits at $118.00, $97.63, and $86.49; resistance stands at $135.04 and $159.53."
           },
