               "summary":  "GE Vernova (GEV) slid 7.53% to $699.33 on November 20, 2026, capping a 41.5% pullback from its $1,195.94 52-week high. The sell-off followed SoftBank’s November 10 disclosure of a $3.73 billion power-procurement charge and Erste Group’s November 18 downgrade to Hold over 2027 order moderation. Yet core fundamentals accelerated in Q3 2026 (October 28), with orders surging 96% organically to $29.3 billion, backlog reaching $197 billion, and full-year free cash flow guidance raised to $16–$17 billion. Technicals are firmly bearish, while the long-term turbine and grid moat stays intact.",
               "technical":  "GE Vernova’s chart as of November 20, 2026 shows a clear bearish breakdown. At $699.33 (down 7.53% on the day), price sits well beneath all three exponential moving averages (EMAs, which track average price trend over 50, 100, and 200 trading days): EMA 50 at $872.56 has already crossed below EMA 200 ($889.22), while EMA 100 remains at $911.14, signaling steep intermediate-term deterioration. Momentum confirms the sell-off: MACD (12/26/9, a trend-momentum gauge) stands at -44.34 below its -37.84 signal line with a -6.50 histogram, indicating persistent downward pressure. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge where under 30 is oversold) fell to 31.98, nearing oversold territory without yet signaling a reversal. Volatility is stretched, with price closing beneath the lower Bollinger Band (a 20-day volatility channel spanning $709.27 to $926.67, midpoint $817.97) and resting in the bottom quarter of its $530.16–$1,195.94 52-week range. Volume hit 6.20 million shares—39% above the 4.47 million 20-day average—confirming heavy institutional selling. Immediate resistance sits near $709–$745 and $818, while the 52-week low at $530.16 anchors distant structural support."
