               "opportunity":  "For long-term AI investors, the pullback (~23% off its $779.82 52-week high) contrasts with an accelerating core business. Advertising revenue grew 26% YoY to $63.06B in Q3 with strong pricing (+14%), and Q4 guidance of $73.0B–$76.0B suggests AI recommendation and ad tools are paying off. Meanwhile, Muse Wonder and ultra-low-cost Muse Ember position Meta to monetize agentic workflows alongside its massive consumer distribution, while Reality Labs losses are projected to narrow from $18B–$19B in 2026 toward ~$15B in 2027. Reclaiming the $616.92 middle band and $638.75–$642.91 EMA cluster would signal technical stabilization.",
               "opportunityCheck":  "The page\u0027s AI-opportunity profile looks mostly accurate, especially keeping bottleneck and pricing power low: Meta is a heavy buyer of chips, power, and cloud capacity rather than a supply-chain chokepoint, as its $135B–$145B 2026 capex plan and November debt sales underscore. However, I would nudge AI revenue exposure slightly higher. AI-driven ad ranking and pricing power propelled Q3 ad revenue up 26% YoY to $63.06B, and Meta now monetizes Superintelligence Labs externally via API pricing on Muse Ember ($0.10 per million input tokens).",
