               "summary":  "Meta Platforms (META) bounced 6.55% on November 23, 2026 to $597.60, yet remains in a bearish technical trend below its 50-, 100-, and 200-day EMAs ($638.75–$642.91). The late-October and November pullback reflects investor anxiety over surging AI capital spending ($34.91B in Q3; $135B–$145B guided for 2026), which turned Q3 free cash flow negative (-$2.12B) and prompted $15B plus €9B in bond issuance. Still, Meta\u0027s consumer network moat looks intact: Q3 revenue grew 26% year-over-year to $64.60B, and Superintelligence Labs shipped Muse Ember on November 9.",
               "technical":  "META closed at $597.60 on November 23, 2026 (+6.55%), yet its chart structure remains bearish. Although its exponential moving averages (EMAs, trend-tracking lines that weight recent prices more heavily) stay tightly stacked in bullish order—EMA 50 at $642.91, EMA 100 at $641.39, and EMA 200 at $638.75—price sits roughly 6%–7% below all three, turning $638.75–$642.91 into heavy overhead resistance. Momentum confirms the downtrend: MACD 12/26/9 (a trend-momentum gauge comparing fast and slow averages) sits at -19.68 below its -17.45 signal line, leaving a negative -2.23 histogram. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) recovered to 41.75, off deeper weakness but still below the neutral 50 mark. Within Bollinger Bands (a volatility envelope around a 20-day average), Monday\u0027s $579.78 low briefly tested the $580.57 lower band as support, while the $616.92 middle band caps near-term rebounds. Trading in the lower third of its $520.26–$779.82 52-week range on below-average volume (19.05M vs. 21.08M 20-day average), this bounce lacks volume conviction."
