               "summary":  "One Stop Systems (OSS) delivered strong operational momentum in late October and November 2026—posting 35.9% Q3 revenue growth to $12.6 million, raising full-year 2026 revenue growth guidance to ~40%, and winning both a $2.75 million U.S. Army contract and a Tier 1 enterprise Gen5 4UP-MAX qualification. Yet the stock trades at USD 4.32, down 79.3% from its USD 20.88 52-week high. The sell-off reflects capital-structure pressure after OSS filed a $50 million at-the-market (ATM) equity offering on October 28 alongside $12.4 million in nine-month operating cash burn and tighter gross margins.",
               "technical":  "As of November 23, 2026, OSS remains in a steep downtrend at USD 4.32, down 79.3% from its USD 20.88 52-week high and just 11.3% above its USD 3.88 52-week low. Price sits far below all three exponential moving averages (EMA — weighted trend averages) in full bearish order: EMA 50 at USD 6.52, EMA 100 at USD 8.39, and EMA 200 at USD 9.23. Inside Bollinger Bands (20/2 — a volatility envelope around the 20-day average), price holds in the lower half below the USD 4.80 midline; immediate support lies at USD 3.88 and the USD 3.38 lower band, with resistance at USD 4.80 and USD 6.23. Momentum signals slowing downside rather than a trend turn: MACD (12/26/9 — a momentum gauge) is deeply negative at -0.70 versus its -0.78 signal line, though a +0.08 histogram shows easing selling pressure, while RSI 14 (a 0–100 gauge; under 30 is oversold) sits weak at 37.24. November 23\u0027s 7.73% bounce faded from a USD 4.63 high on slightly below-average volume (1.62M vs 1.64M), lacking strong confirmation."
